WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:15.000
Run One found a blocking authentication regression, so this candidate can’t move to approval. The next step is remediation—but the failed run remains part of the permanent record.

2
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:30.000
The remediation agent receives the specific finding, the failed candidate, and the same authorization boundary. It’s fixing an observed problem, not quietly redefining the task.

3
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:45.000
When the fix is complete, Arbiter captures a new candidate commit. Candidate One still exists. Candidate Two is a separate immutable change that must earn its own validation result.

4
00:00:45.000 --> 00:01:00.000
Back in Change, we can switch between both candidates. That makes the remediation visible at repository level and prevents the original failure from disappearing behind the latest state.

5
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:15.000
Validation works the same way. Run One remains failed. Run Two is a fresh execution against Candidate Two. Arbiter doesn’t rerun only the check that caught the bug.

6
00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:30.000
The full suite passes on the remediated candidate, while the earlier failure remains visible beside it. That append-only story is what lets a reviewer trust how the result changed.
